    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "According to the American Cancer Society, colorectal cancer (CRC) is the third most commonly diagnosed cancer in men and women the United States, with estimation of 96,000 new cases in 2017. ELF5, an ETS-family transcription factor, has been known to specify cell-fate in the developing embryo, lung, and mammary gland; however, it is not normally expressed highly in the colon, and its role in the colon remains unclear. Here, we investigate ELF5’s role in CRC. Kuo et al. has previously demonstrated that ELF5 exhibits promoter hypomethylation and upregulation in CRC, and that ELF5 overexpression promotes over-proliferation and dysplasia in vitro and increased tumorigenicity in vivo. Based on these findings, we hypothesize that that abnormal upregulation of ELF5 in the colon activates an oncogenic transcriptional network capable of driving tumor progression. We perform ChIP-seq on HCT116, a colon cancer cell line, and HCT116 overexpressing ELF5 to identify binding sites and potential target genes of ELF5 in the HCT116 genome. We show that overexpression of ELF5 results in the increased binding of ELF5 at promoter regions that potentially regulate key genes involved in the PI3K/AKT pathway, p53-mediated apoptosis, and cell cycle control. The data presented here provide novel insight into the mechanism by which overexpression of ELF5 drives tumor progression in the colon. Better understanding of tumor progression from these results may yield new therapeutic strategies for targeting CRC.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
